---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ParticleHero from '../components/ParticleHero.astro';

const base = (import.meta.env.BASE_URL || '/').replace(/\/$/, '') + '/';
---

<Layout title="About | Minh-Ha Nguyen" description="About Minh-Ha Nguyen, PhD candidate in Epidemiology at Vanderbilt, building and evaluating agentic AI systems with the tools of causal inference and experimental design">
  <div class="about-page">
    <ParticleHero name="Minh-Ha Nguyen" subtitle="About" />

    <div class="about-content">
      <section class="intro">
        <p class="lead">
          My work is about one question: when can you trust a conclusion drawn from messy
          data?
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="body">
        <p>
          In pharmacoepidemiology, that means asking whether an apparent drug risk is real or
          an artifact of how the study was designed. In AI, it means asking whether a benchmark
          score measures what we think it does, and whether an agent's conclusion would survive
          the same scrutiny. Different domains, same problem: when should you believe the number?
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="dissertation">
        <h2>Dissertation</h2>
        <p class="dissertation-title">
          Below the Surface: Modeling Hidden Bias for Causal Inference in Large-Scale Health Data
        </p>
        <p>
          My NASA-supported doctoral research asks whether chronic low-dose radiation raises the
          risk of Parkinson's disease among Navy submariners, an earthbound analog for a
          long-duration mission to Mars. The men with the highest exposure also served the
          longest and were often the healthiest to begin with, exactly the kind of selection that
          can hide a real hazard.
        </p>
        <p>
          The three aims bring those hidden assumptions to the surface. The first compares
          non-equivalent ways of defining Parkinson's disease in claims data. The second
          estimates the dose association while separating it from selection into Medicare
          follow-up. The third asks how far an unmeasured confounder, smoking, could move the
          estimate. Throughout, reconstructed radiation dose is treated as a measurement with
          its own error.
        </p>
        <p>
          Language models learn from observational data too: the internet is one enormous
          observational study. The same biases appear when we evaluate them. A test set can be
          selected or leaked, a grader is a reference standard with its own reliability, and a
          score change between two runs can come from the harness as easily as from the model.
          That is the lens I bring to the agents I build,
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.16831" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PIHF-MCP</a>
          and <a href="https://liteodyssey.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">liteOdyssey</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="background">
        <h2>Background</h2>
        <p>
          I came to epidemiology through pharmacy. I trained in pharmacology and clinical
          pharmacy at Queen's University Belfast (BSc, MPharm), then taught pharmacy practice and
          ran machine-learning research on antibiotic stewardship at the University of Medicine
          and Pharmacy at Ho Chi Minh City. As a visiting research fellow in Big Data Epidemiology
          at Yonsei University College of Medicine, I designed nationwide new-user studies of
          prostate drugs and diabetes outcomes.
        </p>
        <p>
          At Vanderbilt my training centers on experimental design, statistical and causal
          inference for observational data, and the scientific computing (Python, R, SAS) that
          large health databases demand. Off the clock I study deep learning, representation
          learning, and reinforcement learning, mostly because I've always been obsessed with
          optimizing things.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="contact">
        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <ul class="contact-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Email:</strong>
            <a href="mailto:ha.m.nguyen@vanderbilt.edu">ha.m.nguyen@vanderbilt.edu</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>CV:</strong>
            <a href={`${base}cv/Minh_Ha_Nguyen_CV.pdf`}>Minh_Ha_Nguyen_CV.pdf</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>GitHub:</strong>
            <a href="https://github.com/minhha0510" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/minhha0510</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .about-page {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1rem 0 3rem;
  }

  .about-page h1 {
    font-size: 2.5rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin: 0 0 2rem 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .about-content section {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .about-content h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 1rem 0;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light);
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    text-align: center;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .about-content p {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .dissertation-title {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .about-content ul {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
  }

  .about-content li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .contact-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .contact-list li {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .about-page h1 {
      font-size: 2rem;
    }

    .lead {
      font-size: 1.1rem;
    }
  }
</style>
